WHY THIS IS A THIRD FILE AND NOT AN EDIT TO `COMMIT`
----------------------------------------------------
  Rule 1 clause 1 forbids writing to anything under third_party/, `COMMIT`
  included, and its rationale is that a local edit voids the provenance
  record. The ruling above lifts that for opcode_table.yaml's bytes and for
  a NEW record; it does not lift it for `COMMIT`. A new file voids nothing:
  `git status --porcelain third_party/` reports the yaml as `M` and this file
  as new, and `COMMIT` is unchanged. This is the shape COMMIT.intrinsics
  (2026-09-19) and third_party/cq_free_pairing/ already took, for the same
  reason.

  It is also forced by the fact COMMIT.intrinsics records: the artefacts pin
  at DIFFERENT revisions and one record cannot say so. `COMMIT` names
  a6a92fe and sha256 6245117d… for the bytes vendored on 2026-08-14, and
  that stays TRUE: every shim, link-witness and partition figure measured
  between 2026-08-14 and 2026-10-04 was measured against those bytes.
  Overwriting its "Pinned revision" line would erase that record; adding a
  second one would put two pins in one file — the shape a drift check
  cannot read.

  `COMMIT`'s "Refresh command" also names a path CQ_lang no longer lives at
  (/Users/sorenwilkening/Desktop/CQ_lang) and a working-tree `cp`. Neither
  can be corrected there under Rule 1. The command below is this file's.

